1
00:00:12,177 --> 00:00:18,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Majjhima Nikaya 44, the short-term classification.

2
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:25,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at one time, the Buddha was staying near Rajagaha in the bamboo grove.

3
00:00:26,965 --> 00:00:31,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the layman, Visakha, went to the nun, Damodina.

4
00:00:32,007 --> 00:00:40,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Bab sat down to one side and said to her, noble lady, they speak of a thing called identity.

5
00:00:41,672 --> 00:00:44,493
[SPEAKER_00]: And that identity is, the Pali word is sakaya.

6
00:00:45,654 --> 00:00:52,197
[SPEAKER_00]: So, some people will translate that as person, or embodiment.

7
00:00:52,337 --> 00:00:52,537
[SPEAKER_00]: I.V.

8
00:00:52,557 --> 00:00:52,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Horner.

9
00:00:54,177 --> 00:00:55,358
[SPEAKER_00]: No, another jyoti.

10
00:00:56,381 --> 00:01:02,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, noble lady, they speak of this thing called identity.

11
00:01:04,564 --> 00:01:07,305
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this identity that the Buddha spoke of?

12
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:11,327
[SPEAKER_00]: The Sakaya.

13
00:01:11,367 --> 00:01:16,329
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha said that these five assumed aggregates are Sakaya.

14
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:24,192
[SPEAKER_00]: That is form, feeling, perception, saṅkharas, determinations and consciousness.

15
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:36,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying... a good... Risāka approved and agreed with Dhammadhīnā, then he asked another question.

16
00:01:38,164 --> 00:01:40,845
[SPEAKER_00]: They speak of this thing called the origin of identity.

17
00:01:43,146 --> 00:01:44,587
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the origin of identity?

18
00:01:45,347 --> 00:01:55,752
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the craving that leads to future rebirth, mixed up with relishing, delight and lust, taking pleasure in various different realms.

19
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:01,602
[SPEAKER_00]: That is craving for sensual pleasures, craving for being, craving for non-being.

20
00:02:04,623 --> 00:02:12,787
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said that that that is the origin of Sakaya, identity, what we call the person.

21
00:02:17,149 --> 00:02:20,670
[SPEAKER_00]: So what is what is... what would you say?

22
00:02:21,231 --> 00:02:22,851
[SPEAKER_00]: How would you explain identity?

23
00:02:23,351 --> 00:02:24,432
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this identity?

24
00:02:26,641 --> 00:02:27,862
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... what am I?

25
00:02:28,963 --> 00:02:29,704
[SPEAKER_00]: What is myself?

26
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:36,750
[SPEAKER_01]: What is me?

27
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:42,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whatever... whatever you regard yourself to be, however vaguely...

28
00:02:44,416 --> 00:02:53,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any notion of I am as a... as a... as an individual, pretty much, that's already...

29
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:55,882
[SPEAKER_01]: But you take identity to be for you.

30
00:02:56,642 --> 00:02:58,044
[SPEAKER_01]: So, personality.

31
00:02:58,804 --> 00:03:01,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is... it is a... it is a general term.

32
00:03:01,827 --> 00:03:03,488
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a specific personality.

33
00:03:03,648 --> 00:03:05,630
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the fact that personality is there.

34
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:08,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Personality of a subject.

35
00:03:09,513 --> 00:03:12,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no sense of identity without a sense of...

36
00:03:12,495 --> 00:03:13,155
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a subject.

37
00:03:14,196 --> 00:03:21,282
[SPEAKER_01]: And, in other words, there is then no sense of identity without a separation between you and the objects of your world.

38
00:03:24,576 --> 00:03:26,157
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is not the problem.

39
00:03:26,197 --> 00:03:27,559
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the result of the problem.

40
00:03:27,959 --> 00:03:29,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem of craving towards the five aggregates.

41
00:03:31,262 --> 00:03:33,244
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where it comes about, the arising.

42
00:03:34,084 --> 00:03:36,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that's where it says the origin of personality.

43
00:03:36,567 --> 00:03:42,292
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the origin of personality was not, or rather, is not contained solely in the past.

44
00:03:42,772 --> 00:03:47,816
[SPEAKER_01]: The origin of personality is the fact that craving is still there, was there, will be there.

45
00:03:48,857 --> 00:03:51,580
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as craving is there, personality is maintained.

46
00:03:56,442 --> 00:04:05,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And craving is the most immediately manifested through sense of ownership.

47
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:19,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, like, when pleasant feeling arises, if you are not assuming it to be for me, mine, pertaining to me, affecting me, me, me, me, me, me, you wouldn't be craving for more of it.

48
00:04:21,262 --> 00:04:41,750
[SPEAKER_01]: When the unpleasant feeling arises, again, against me, eh... mind that I don't want it to be, and all these... all these other kind of permutations, eh... that's why you end up craving for it to not be, because you feel... entitled to do so, so to speak.

49
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:43,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel it's... it's your right to do so.

50
00:04:43,551 --> 00:04:48,453
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your right to defend yourself against these feelings, and it's your right to prolong the other feelings that you want.

51
00:04:49,913 --> 00:04:52,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through that craving, you maintain your sense of ownership.

52
00:04:54,475 --> 00:04:55,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratuitous.

53
00:04:55,135 --> 00:05:01,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, things present themselves as towards you, and you assume that you are because, look, these things have a reason for me.

54
00:05:03,038 --> 00:05:09,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's that's exactly how you end up maintaining the notion of identity, of I am such and such.

55
00:05:09,381 --> 00:05:18,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Any notions of personality that you have, are the symptoms of your underlying ownership of things, of your feelings, of your perceptions, of your intentions.

56
00:05:20,254 --> 00:05:22,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to undo the personality, you undo the ownership.

57
00:05:23,695 --> 00:05:27,375
[SPEAKER_01]: But you... you can't undo the ownership as a... as a matter of choice.

58
00:05:27,655 --> 00:05:30,576
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't decide to not own things, because you own that.

59
00:05:31,556 --> 00:05:34,517
[SPEAKER_01]: You will own your renunciation of things.

60
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:41,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when people think that all they need to do is throw away their possessions and they'll be free from attachments.

61
00:05:41,898 --> 00:05:42,078
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

62
00:05:42,158 --> 00:05:43,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Attachments were never in the possessions.

63
00:05:44,019 --> 00:05:45,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Ownership is not in the things.

64
00:05:45,339 --> 00:05:46,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the aggregates.

65
00:05:47,527 --> 00:05:57,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the assumption of ownership is basically in regard to the aggregates, but it's not in them, and it cannot be found anywhere else apart from it, as the Buddha described, upādāna.

66
00:06:02,079 --> 00:06:04,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be trying to get rid of the personality.

67
00:06:06,222 --> 00:06:10,866
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be trying to uproot the ownership of it.

68
00:06:12,327 --> 00:06:14,709
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, even the notion of personality is regarded as mine.

69
00:06:19,148 --> 00:06:34,151
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even if you don't have, you don't take the personality personal, you don't own the personality, there is still, even the Buddha was, is still a... Yeah, there's a different Pali word for that.

70
00:06:34,211 --> 00:06:34,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Pūgala.

71
00:06:35,211 --> 00:06:37,012
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the individual underneath there.

72
00:06:37,472 --> 00:06:38,532
[SPEAKER_01]: There's still an individual.

73
00:06:38,552 --> 00:06:43,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have an individual without personality, but you can have a personality without an individual.

74
00:06:44,632 --> 00:06:48,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, individual is basically a person freed from personality.

75
00:06:48,794 --> 00:06:54,317
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Pughala is used to describe arahants as well, noble disciples.

76
00:06:54,858 --> 00:06:55,998
[SPEAKER_01]: It literally means an individual.

77
00:06:57,279 --> 00:07:05,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And basically, individual is what is left when personality has been freed from ownership.

78
00:07:05,644 --> 00:07:07,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And what is then left?

79
00:07:08,325 --> 00:07:09,766
[SPEAKER_00]: As an individual, what is there?

80
00:07:12,105 --> 00:07:12,365
[SPEAKER_00]: What is...?

81
00:07:13,366 --> 00:07:14,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of ownership.

82
00:07:14,386 --> 00:07:14,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolute.

83
00:07:14,786 --> 00:07:16,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is still there is the aggregates.

84
00:07:17,408 --> 00:07:22,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but those aggregates are not the aggregates that Puthujjana can relate to, for as long as he's a Puthujjana.

85
00:07:22,611 --> 00:07:28,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say that it's the same aggregates that Puthujjana owns, and then it's those aggregates that are left for an arahant.

86
00:07:29,314 --> 00:07:35,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true in theory, but practically, nobody can have an impartial view on Puthujjana and arahant spectrum.

87
00:07:36,238 --> 00:07:38,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be pertaining to some of it.

88
00:07:38,139 --> 00:07:40,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're either a Puthujjana, or you're close to

89
00:07:41,673 --> 00:07:45,676
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, when you speak about the five aggregates, it's going to be accurate what you refer to.

90
00:07:47,337 --> 00:07:50,940
[SPEAKER_01]: But that should not then be used the same to describe Puthujjana's five aggregates.

91
00:07:51,821 --> 00:07:53,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not the same.

92
00:07:53,362 --> 00:07:58,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's said, from Puthujjana's point of view, basically, the five aggregates cease, for another hand.

93
00:07:58,646 --> 00:08:01,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Cessation of form, cessation of consciousness, as the Buddha described it.

94
00:08:01,789 --> 00:08:05,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, it's utter cessation of the aggregates that Puthujjana knows of.

95
00:08:07,238 --> 00:08:08,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind aggregates.

96
00:08:08,239 --> 00:08:08,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

97
00:08:09,019 --> 00:08:10,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Those are the only aggregates he has.

98
00:08:10,701 --> 00:08:15,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Aggregates that exist through the sense of mind.

99
00:08:16,786 --> 00:08:20,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Assumed aggregates and not assumed aggregates.

100
00:08:20,549 --> 00:08:20,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

101
00:08:21,009 --> 00:08:23,091
[SPEAKER_00]: But you only know assumed aggregates.

102
00:08:23,211 --> 00:08:23,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

103
00:08:23,431 --> 00:08:30,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see them, like, shoulder to shoulder and compare them and then sort of pick and choose or contemplate them rightly.

104
00:08:31,618 --> 00:08:33,219
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to be affected by them.

105
00:08:37,883 --> 00:08:40,223
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by seeing that you're affected by them, how do you...?

106
00:08:40,543 --> 00:08:46,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know you're affected by them because you're liable to suffering.

107
00:08:49,525 --> 00:08:53,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Your personality is your burden.

108
00:08:55,286 --> 00:08:58,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just the image of yourself that you have in others, but also just in general.

109
00:08:59,327 --> 00:09:05,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that can come your way, things that you don't want, things that can go away from you, that you want to keep.

110
00:09:06,308 --> 00:09:06,988
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things.

111
00:09:07,835 --> 00:09:10,637
[SPEAKER_01]: are kind of relationships of your personality.

112
00:09:12,218 --> 00:09:18,862
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're all symptoms of the underlying problem, which is kind of, overall, the most general symptom of that problem is suffering.

113
00:09:20,062 --> 00:09:21,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You're liable to suffering.

114
00:09:23,484 --> 00:09:30,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're freeing yourself from suffering, you will be freeing yourself from personality and all these other symptoms as well, because you're freeing yourself from the underlying cause of it.

115
00:09:31,049 --> 00:09:32,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Underlying cause being craving.

116
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:36,732
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you need to recognize the problem.

117
00:09:38,982 --> 00:09:43,144
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is not that your person is like... personality is like this and... or like that.

118
00:09:43,764 --> 00:09:48,105
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that personality and liability to suffering go hand in hand.

119
00:09:52,247 --> 00:09:57,169
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just two aspects of the same underlying cause, two different symptoms.

120
00:09:58,969 --> 00:10:03,591
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you recognize that that's the problem, you'll stop blaming personality to be the problem.

121
00:10:05,025 --> 00:10:09,588
[SPEAKER_01]: And then try to engage in, like, psychotherapy or whatever, try to rewrite your psychological patterns.

122
00:10:09,608 --> 00:10:10,428
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all management.

123
00:10:11,089 --> 00:10:14,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all just trying to re-tailor the symptoms the way you think they should be.

124
00:10:14,831 --> 00:10:17,152
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a symptom of something else.

125
00:10:18,133 --> 00:10:20,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Something else, as the sutta says, being the craving.

126
00:10:22,202 --> 00:10:25,664
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about denying the personality, trying to get rid of it.

127
00:10:25,844 --> 00:10:31,988
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about seeing it as a symptom of an underlying cause and using it then to discern the underlying cause.

128
00:10:32,368 --> 00:10:43,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Because with fading away of your ownership of your personality, as in with fading away of those symptoms, you know then the underlying cause of the craving has been diminished.

129
00:10:48,864 --> 00:10:58,829
[SPEAKER_01]: But you'll never be able to see that way for as long as your practice is about denying your personality, denying the sense of self, and just arguing the point, oh, there's no self.

130
00:10:58,849 --> 00:10:59,829
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha said there's no self.

131
00:10:59,990 --> 00:11:00,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, everything is changing.

132
00:11:01,030 --> 00:11:01,730
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no self.

133
00:11:02,251 --> 00:11:07,313
[SPEAKER_00]: There's also, you know, you might not like yourself, because you're angry.

134
00:11:07,613 --> 00:11:07,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

135
00:11:08,014 --> 00:11:08,714
[SPEAKER_00]: You're lustful.

136
00:11:08,954 --> 00:11:09,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

137
00:11:09,114 --> 00:11:09,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of bad habits.

138
00:11:10,335 --> 00:11:10,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

139
00:11:10,655 --> 00:11:14,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to change those habits, but you don't want to change yourself.

140
00:11:15,709 --> 00:11:21,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, as I said, you don't want to... you don't want to be aiming your efforts at changing that sense of self, because that's a symptom.

141
00:11:21,772 --> 00:11:36,059
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be aiming your efforts at restraining the outpours of that sense of self, which are kind of aggravating the underlying cause, that's then amplifying your sense of self, as in increasing your conceit, increasing your self-centeredness, increasing your selfishness, increasing your hate.

142
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:37,400
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things.

143
00:11:39,081 --> 00:11:43,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And those things are maintained and proliferated through your actions, through choices you make.

144
00:11:47,632 --> 00:11:52,934
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that it's rooted in choices means it's on you to choose, not to decide beforehand.

145
00:11:55,994 --> 00:12:02,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever there is a choice for wholesome or unwholesome, you choose wholesome, you don't choose unwholesome.

146
00:12:03,036 --> 00:12:03,957
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your effort.

147
00:12:06,277 --> 00:12:09,438
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't welcome unwholesome, you don't entertain it, you don't engage with it.

148
00:12:10,678 --> 00:12:16,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you basically prohibit craving to spread.

149
00:12:20,259 --> 00:12:23,441
[SPEAKER_01]: With craving diminishing, sense of self is diminishing.

150
00:12:23,521 --> 00:12:24,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Your conceit is diminishing.

151
00:12:25,182 --> 00:12:25,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Indirectly.

152
00:12:26,663 --> 00:12:29,645
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say to this extent, I'm less conceited.

153
00:12:32,287 --> 00:12:33,848
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only know that in hindsight.

154
00:12:34,568 --> 00:12:41,233
[SPEAKER_00]: When you stop, when you start choosing what you haven't been choosing, when you start choosing wholesome instead of unwholesome.

155
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:44,275
[SPEAKER_01]: When you start choosing sense restraint instead of sensuality.

156
00:12:44,795 --> 00:12:46,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not pleasant.

157
00:12:46,136 --> 00:12:46,856
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

158
00:12:47,116 --> 00:12:47,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially.

159
00:12:47,597 --> 00:12:49,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that is who you are.

160
00:12:49,939 --> 00:13:09,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah,

161
00:13:16,956 --> 00:13:19,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's, you know, when anxiety can come in, and doubt, and stuff.

162
00:13:19,758 --> 00:13:22,741
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, all those things are another phenomena risen.

163
00:13:24,682 --> 00:13:26,904
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your personality is phenomena risen.

164
00:13:26,964 --> 00:13:28,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you use those things as symptoms.

165
00:13:28,945 --> 00:13:33,689
[SPEAKER_01]: As symptoms that, oh, okay, I am actually delving in the right direction.

166
00:13:34,910 --> 00:13:39,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm not... if I were not feeling anxious about undoing my sense of identity, my belongings,

167
00:13:40,303 --> 00:13:43,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I'm not clearly dealing with the underlying cause of those things.

168
00:13:44,006 --> 00:13:49,670
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that I am feeling anxious, the fact that I am feeling doubtful, means I am actually, at least, going in the right direction.

169
00:13:49,990 --> 00:13:51,231
[SPEAKER_01]: How long I have to do it, I don't know.

170
00:13:51,632 --> 00:13:52,812
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the faith comes in.

171
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:56,295
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's more of a faith in finding the cure.

172
00:13:57,216 --> 00:13:59,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in faith of some magical release.

173
00:14:02,220 --> 00:14:09,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, many similes in the suttas, but obviously the most obvious one is that, like, the physician gives you the medicine that's unpleasant to take.

174
00:14:11,074 --> 00:14:17,738
[SPEAKER_01]: But out of the faith in physician and out of certain discernment of your own symptoms, you get to see the symptoms are diminishing.

175
00:14:19,298 --> 00:14:25,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And you keep taking the bitter medicine or the painful medicine, the restraint and so on, but the symptoms are diminishing.

176
00:14:25,102 --> 00:14:33,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, actually, not just that you will not mind the unpleasant medicine, you will look forward to it, because once it heals you, you won't need any of it.

177
00:14:35,967 --> 00:14:37,288
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite... that can be painful.

178
00:14:37,769 --> 00:14:38,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

179
00:14:38,869 --> 00:14:44,114
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not always painful, but when it is, you don't make a problem out of it.

180
00:14:46,075 --> 00:14:49,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sense restraint is painful only when sense desire is there.

181
00:14:49,758 --> 00:14:52,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's painful because of the sense desire, not because of the restraint.

182
00:14:53,821 --> 00:15:01,908
[SPEAKER_01]: If sense... say, if sense desire is not there, you not engaging with sense objects is not painful, because you don't want to engage with it.

183
00:15:01,968 --> 00:15:04,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the discrepancy that was causing the pain is gone.

184
00:15:06,019 --> 00:15:12,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the question is just not to not give in to the pain and act out of it, as in try to get rid of it by giving in to centrality.

185
00:15:14,581 --> 00:15:28,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all these things would be like rounding up your sense of your sakāyā, your personality view, that you are an independent person, independent of this experience, independent of the choices you make.

186
00:15:28,824 --> 00:15:30,605
[SPEAKER_01]: You're an entity that exists on its own.

187
00:15:31,571 --> 00:15:33,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You will see that that's not the case.

188
00:15:33,632 --> 00:15:39,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are an entity that exists on its own, why do you feel anxiety, if there are things that are doubtful or threatening to you?

189
00:15:41,057 --> 00:15:47,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You would be fully enclosed within yourself, impermeable to anything external, yet you're fully affected emotionally right from your core.

190
00:15:49,141 --> 00:15:51,823
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, people who are not restrained,

191
00:15:52,536 --> 00:16:00,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And don't practice mindfulness despite all the... all the kind of evidence of anxiety, non-control, suffering.

192
00:16:00,522 --> 00:16:07,647
[SPEAKER_01]: They still think they're an entity fully in charge of themselves, independent of the whole world and everything that happens to them.

193
00:16:08,187 --> 00:16:10,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, independent, not permeated by the world.

194
00:16:10,829 --> 00:16:11,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

195
00:16:11,409 --> 00:16:16,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's like Sakaya... Sakaya Deity, basically the... the whole, like, personality view.

196
00:16:18,905 --> 00:16:29,390
[SPEAKER_01]: The view that there is this personality of me, I am, whatever they might be, it's not... it's obviously not even... cannot be specific, I'm such and such, that's... you decide that upon your person's sense of personality.

197
00:16:29,990 --> 00:16:34,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that sense of personality is a view that this sense of me is completely independent of everything else.

198
00:16:36,053 --> 00:16:39,634
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Sakaya and Atavada, it's just hand in hand.

199
00:16:39,734 --> 00:16:40,915
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing, pretty much.

200
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:58,153
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you start raising sensory strength, start sort of digging up these mistaken foundations of your sakāyā, you will see that your sakāyā cannot be independent from the world you experience, from the five aggregates.

201
00:17:01,194 --> 00:17:05,095
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will stop maintaining such personality view.

202
00:17:06,392 --> 00:17:08,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when a person develops the right view, basically.

203
00:17:08,795 --> 00:17:12,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, that right view doesn't fit in with the wrong view.

204
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:13,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

205
00:17:13,521 --> 00:17:14,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mutually exclusive, yeah.

206
00:17:33,304 --> 00:17:36,947
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is from the Bhikkhuni Samhita 10.

207
00:17:37,507 --> 00:17:39,289
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know the exact number.

208
00:17:41,871 --> 00:17:48,576
[SPEAKER_00]: And here we have Mara, the evil one, speaking to a nun, the nun Vajira.

209
00:17:49,677 --> 00:17:53,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Mara says to her, by whom is this creature formed?

210
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:55,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is this?

211
00:17:55,541 --> 00:17:57,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the creature's maker?

212
00:17:58,103 --> 00:17:59,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the risen creature?

213
00:17:59,804 --> 00:18:01,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Who is the creature that ceases?

214
00:18:02,727 --> 00:18:08,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And Vajira the nun says, why do you refer to the creature, Mara?

215
00:18:08,352 --> 00:18:09,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you involved in wrong view?

216
00:18:11,235 --> 00:18:14,739
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a pile of pure determinations.

217
00:18:14,839 --> 00:18:17,722
[SPEAKER_00]: There is here no creature to be found.

218
00:18:18,899 --> 00:18:23,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as for an assemblage of parts, there is the term, a chariot.

219
00:18:24,121 --> 00:18:28,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there are the aggregates, convention says, a creature.

220
00:18:28,282 --> 00:18:30,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

221
00:18:30,483 --> 00:18:32,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Or... but it's the palatum sata, but...

222
00:18:33,424 --> 00:18:41,126
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... the creature, the palatum sata, it's closer to palatum pūgala, then it's close... then... then it's closer to sakāya.

223
00:18:42,347 --> 00:18:45,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... it's closer to the individual, rather than the person.

224
00:18:46,348 --> 00:18:49,130
[SPEAKER_01]: So, an individual that's appropriate in his mind is what a person is.

225
00:18:49,150 --> 00:18:51,913
[SPEAKER_01]: A creature that's appropriate in his mind is where the question comes up.

226
00:18:51,993 --> 00:18:52,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Who then?

227
00:18:53,034 --> 00:18:54,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Who is the owner of it?

228
00:18:54,195 --> 00:18:54,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Of this person.

229
00:18:55,296 --> 00:18:56,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Of this... Yeah, exactly.

230
00:18:57,197 --> 00:18:58,018
[SPEAKER_01]: This person.

231
00:18:58,178 --> 00:19:01,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like the Mahārāja there said, that's why she asked, are you involved in the wrong view?

232
00:19:01,881 --> 00:19:11,169
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the fact that he asked, who is the owner of the individual, means that he puts the personality view first, and then individual second.

233
00:19:11,930 --> 00:19:13,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the trait of the wrong view.

234
00:19:15,455 --> 00:19:19,678
[SPEAKER_01]: You think things are yours because you are, but it's the other way around.

235
00:19:20,258 --> 00:19:22,959
[SPEAKER_01]: You are because you keep taking things to be yours.

236
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:25,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

237
00:19:25,281 --> 00:19:30,424
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be an individual without being a person, but you can't be a person without being an individual.

238
00:19:31,544 --> 00:19:32,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have a sense of self.

239
00:19:34,286 --> 00:19:40,650
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have a sense of self without taking things as mine, but you can be taking things as mine without going to the extent of the sense of self.

240
00:19:41,800 --> 00:19:45,823
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the Sutlej concedes for Arya Sāvaka to abandon later on in the practice.

241
00:19:47,244 --> 00:19:51,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but the appropriation comes first.

242
00:19:51,107 --> 00:19:52,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of self is the symptom of it.

243
00:19:52,809 --> 00:19:53,649
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the result of it.

244
00:19:54,850 --> 00:19:58,113
[SPEAKER_01]: But Mara asks, who is the owner of appropriation?

245
00:19:58,553 --> 00:20:01,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means he puts the sense of self first, again.

246
00:20:01,836 --> 00:20:06,660
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that she says, the creature is only a pile of determinations, means pile of choices.

247
00:20:08,706 --> 00:20:10,107
[SPEAKER_01]: The creature is a pile of choices.

248
00:20:11,227 --> 00:20:17,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, wrong choices result in that creature having a self-view and thinking that its own self is independent of his own choices.

249
00:20:18,091 --> 00:20:19,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Choices of ownership.

250
00:20:20,092 --> 00:20:23,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, things are mine because I am.

251
00:20:24,054 --> 00:20:32,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the choices were right of that creature, if choices were not choices based on the craving, he will undo that view that he's independent of his own choices.

252
00:20:32,799 --> 00:20:35,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he'll see it's just choices arising, choices ceasing.

253
00:20:36,145 --> 00:20:38,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Determination is manifesting, determination is disappearing.

254
00:20:39,727 --> 00:20:40,808
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are you talking about?

255
00:20:43,030 --> 00:20:45,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the assemblage of the parts of the chariot is real as such.

256
00:20:46,972 --> 00:20:57,279
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about denying the assemblage, saying the creature doesn't exist because it's made of parts, which is how the wrong view takes it, and certainly modern interpretations.

257
00:20:58,203 --> 00:20:58,323
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

258
00:20:58,343 --> 00:21:01,064
[SPEAKER_01]: The creature is the name of the parts.

259
00:21:01,464 --> 00:21:06,805
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no need to deny the creature and say chariot is not real because it's made out of components.

260
00:21:07,945 --> 00:21:14,287
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you even know what those components are of if you don't have the unifying identity of it as a chariot?

261
00:21:15,447 --> 00:21:15,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

262
00:21:15,727 --> 00:21:18,848
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no unifying identity of an individual, i.e.

263
00:21:18,988 --> 00:21:23,069
[SPEAKER_01]: the five aggregates, how can you know the five aggregates are part of the individual?

264
00:21:23,169 --> 00:21:23,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You couldn't.

265
00:21:24,610 --> 00:21:38,097
[SPEAKER_01]: But people hope whereby if you sort of... they hope that you'll be able to escape the ownership of the creature by denying its existence through saying that it's made of parts, thus it's not real.

266
00:21:38,438 --> 00:21:39,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Parts are real.

267
00:21:40,579 --> 00:21:43,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how can parts not be real while you're talking about?

268
00:21:45,602 --> 00:21:51,365
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm referring to is... it's based, I think, it's based on that verse that the whole the questions of the King Milinda.

269
00:21:53,486 --> 00:22:03,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And the monk Nagasena, when he tried to basically argue that there is no self because it's made out of parts, said, personality, sakāyā, is made out of parts, that's why it's not real.

270
00:22:03,633 --> 00:22:04,094
[SPEAKER_01]: It is real.

271
00:22:04,494 --> 00:22:05,675
[SPEAKER_01]: And she says that it's real.

272
00:22:07,016 --> 00:22:08,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Says, convention calls its creature.

273
00:22:08,857 --> 00:22:13,541
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the name of this particular assemblage of parts, which is you as a human being.

274
00:22:14,521 --> 00:22:15,382
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not the problem.

275
00:22:15,422 --> 00:22:17,264
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is you applying ownership

276
00:22:18,025 --> 00:22:23,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Maintaining ownership that then implies the self-view, as in view independent of the assemblage of the parts.

277
00:22:24,831 --> 00:22:27,353
[SPEAKER_01]: But even such mistaken view is real.

278
00:22:30,956 --> 00:22:31,556
[SPEAKER_01]: As a mistake.

279
00:22:32,317 --> 00:22:37,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you keep going, if you go around denying the mistake from being there, how can you resolve it?

280
00:22:38,802 --> 00:22:39,523
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there's no mistake.

281
00:22:39,843 --> 00:22:40,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Mistake is not real.

282
00:22:42,085 --> 00:22:44,087
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there for you then to resolve?

283
00:22:46,234 --> 00:22:47,155
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a mistake.

284
00:22:47,335 --> 00:22:48,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is suffering.

285
00:22:49,096 --> 00:22:50,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Means there is a self of you which is a mistake.

286
00:22:51,778 --> 00:22:53,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So you must acknowledge it first.

287
00:22:56,722 --> 00:22:59,144
[SPEAKER_01]: And that acknowledgement is the recognition of the wrong view.

288
00:22:59,824 --> 00:23:01,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what she asked Mara.

289
00:23:03,107 --> 00:23:12,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on his question, implied, like, clearly reveals that he puts that sense of identity first as the owner of the creature.

290
00:23:12,674 --> 00:23:15,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Who is this creature formed by?

291
00:23:16,773 --> 00:23:20,777
[SPEAKER_01]: As if there is the entity independent of this creature.

292
00:23:22,278 --> 00:23:23,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Creature is second to the entity.

293
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:26,682
[SPEAKER_01]: But the right view realizes that it isn't.

294
00:23:26,922 --> 00:23:27,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the first.

295
00:23:27,723 --> 00:23:32,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Creature is the basis for your sense of entity, for your identity.

296
00:23:32,707 --> 00:23:35,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Creature is the basis for the personality view.

297
00:23:35,930 --> 00:23:36,230
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

298
00:23:36,410 --> 00:23:37,991
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said in the previous sutta.

299
00:23:38,011 --> 00:23:38,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's through craving.

300
00:23:39,413 --> 00:23:43,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Through craving in regard to things that creature has access to, basically.

301
00:23:44,183 --> 00:23:57,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Through craving in regard to the five aggregates, through craving in regard to the feelings of the creature, you get to develop the notion that you, as an identity, is independent of this creature.

302
00:24:00,294 --> 00:24:01,795
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm so much more than this body.

303
00:24:01,835 --> 00:24:03,316
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm so much more than this mind.

304
00:24:05,338 --> 00:24:08,280
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know that, except on the basis of your body and mind?

305
00:24:10,120 --> 00:24:11,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I meant before.

306
00:24:11,140 --> 00:24:16,462
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep assuming it as outside of the creature, as opposed to contain within the creature.

307
00:24:16,662 --> 00:24:23,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that self-view that is independent, it's...

308
00:24:24,024 --> 00:24:24,384
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

309
00:24:25,265 --> 00:24:31,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But, well, that wrong self-view is infinite, in a sense.

310
00:24:31,447 --> 00:24:31,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Eternal.

311
00:24:33,275 --> 00:24:33,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

312
00:24:34,136 --> 00:24:34,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

313
00:24:34,516 --> 00:24:35,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So big.

314
00:24:35,376 --> 00:24:41,440
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, that's why sense of being eternal and self-view go hand in hand.

315
00:24:42,020 --> 00:24:47,864
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have, you can't have a self-view without implicit notion of it will last forever, eternally.

316
00:24:48,604 --> 00:24:53,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any notion of lasting and cessation is on the level of the creature and the aggregates and the feelings.

317
00:24:54,128 --> 00:24:59,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But you assume yourself to be independent of that, thus you automatically assume yourself to be eternal.

318
00:25:00,711 --> 00:25:09,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you seeing the notion of eternity is within the creature, is you basically removing the basis for that gratuitous mistake that you keep maintaining.

319
00:25:10,479 --> 00:25:14,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Mistake of I am independent of the experience as a whole.

320
00:25:14,142 --> 00:25:15,783
[SPEAKER_01]: I am independent of these five aggregates.

321
00:25:17,625 --> 00:25:24,090
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm within that... You are fully within because you cannot occur anywhere else except on that basis, which means you're second to it.

322
00:25:24,991 --> 00:25:27,053
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the reduction of Atavāda.

323
00:25:27,293 --> 00:25:28,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Atavāda is assuming that

324
00:25:29,837 --> 00:25:38,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you have become unable to assume that anymore, and you will become so, if you keep

325
00:25:39,522 --> 00:25:45,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Rectifying your own, basically, mistake of assuming it to be separate.

326
00:25:45,387 --> 00:25:47,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you assume to be separate, you don't deny it.

327
00:25:47,950 --> 00:25:48,971
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your starting point.

328
00:25:49,691 --> 00:25:55,557
[SPEAKER_01]: But you now include the assumption of separation within the basis of the presently enduring experience as a whole.

329
00:25:56,257 --> 00:26:02,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... you will rectify the mistake, not by denying it or trying to get rid of it, but by seeing the bigger picture that includes it.

330
00:26:04,718 --> 00:26:08,960
[SPEAKER_01]: The bigger picture being, it doesn't matter how much you conceive yourself to be independent.

331
00:26:09,560 --> 00:26:16,703
[SPEAKER_01]: Any notion of independence, any notion of self, is within the present enduring experience, for which you have no say whatsoever.

332
00:26:17,584 --> 00:26:27,668
[SPEAKER_01]: And you keep practicing that kind of contemplation, that kind of mental training and drilling, of whenever there is a sense of self, whenever there is a sense of I am, it's not about, oh, I'm not real.

333
00:26:27,888 --> 00:26:29,709
[SPEAKER_01]: I am real, but I am within this.

334
00:26:30,109 --> 00:26:31,390
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not within I am.

335
00:26:33,243 --> 00:26:39,486
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like the Arya Savaka's practice of not I am, not for me.

336
00:26:40,747 --> 00:26:44,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I am not this and this is not for me, how they usually translate it.

337
00:26:44,849 --> 00:26:48,812
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually not is the first, I am is the second.

338
00:26:49,772 --> 00:26:51,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being the next, yeah, within.

339
00:26:51,453 --> 00:26:51,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

340
00:26:51,853 --> 00:26:53,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am is within not.

341
00:26:54,515 --> 00:26:55,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine is within not.

342
00:26:56,836 --> 00:26:58,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Self is within not.

343
00:27:03,992 --> 00:27:05,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you stop owning it.

344
00:27:06,293 --> 00:27:09,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how your personality will just become an individual.

345
00:27:09,634 --> 00:27:15,197
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why an arahant cannot be distinguished by a patujana, because all he sees is personalities.

346
00:27:15,997 --> 00:27:21,179
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't see an individual through the personality, because for him, individual is personality.

347
00:27:21,940 --> 00:27:28,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when arahant speaks, talks, decides, tells him off, shouts, he'll think, oh, he's an angry monk or whatever.

348
00:27:28,403 --> 00:27:31,104
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a patujana, as there were cases in the suttas.

349
00:27:32,032 --> 00:27:33,674
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we say, no, he's completely enlightened.

350
00:27:34,375 --> 00:27:42,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't tell it externally apart, because he still has choices, preferences, and I don't want that, I want this, because that was never the problem.

351
00:27:43,747 --> 00:27:49,575
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem was he maintaining the assumption of basically an entity separate from all of that.

352
00:27:53,404 --> 00:27:54,705
[SPEAKER_00]: The verse continues a little bit.

353
00:27:54,765 --> 00:27:56,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go back a bit.

354
00:27:56,347 --> 00:27:59,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as an assemblage of parts, there is a term chariot.

355
00:28:00,010 --> 00:28:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when there are aggregates, convention says a creature.

356
00:28:03,773 --> 00:28:03,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

357
00:28:04,113 --> 00:28:05,655
[SPEAKER_01]: So, aggregates are called a creature.

358
00:28:07,036 --> 00:28:07,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

359
00:28:09,138 --> 00:28:09,798
[SPEAKER_00]: The assemblage.

360
00:28:09,878 --> 00:28:11,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

361
00:28:11,460 --> 00:28:14,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter if it's one part, five parts, fifteen parts.

362
00:28:15,503 --> 00:28:17,425
[SPEAKER_01]: The name of the parts is the creature.

363
00:28:18,106 --> 00:28:20,448
[SPEAKER_01]: The name of the parts of the vehicle is chariot.

364
00:28:21,253 --> 00:28:25,694
[SPEAKER_00]: But then she continues, she says, it is merely suffering that comes into being.

365
00:28:26,514 --> 00:28:32,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering that stands and... well, stands and disappears.

366
00:28:33,416 --> 00:28:35,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing apart from suffering comes into being.

367
00:28:35,396 --> 00:28:37,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing other than suffering ceases.

368
00:28:38,197 --> 00:28:41,818
[SPEAKER_00]: So, she's seen the mistake in... She's seen the mistake in what's left now.

369
00:28:41,858 --> 00:28:50,540
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically just seeing, like, when I said just earlier on, discrepancy of that ownership means suffering.

370
00:28:52,627 --> 00:29:10,780
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you... once you have undone the extent of that discrepancy, as in you don't assume anymore that you are an entity independent of your experience as a whole, independent of anything you can see, perceive, feel and intend, you realize such thing is inconceivable.

371
00:29:11,281 --> 00:29:14,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your sense of self is within those things that are not in your control.

372
00:29:14,223 --> 00:29:20,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, your sense of self starts to fade as the master owner of the experience, which means discrepancy begins to lessen.

373
00:29:23,593 --> 00:29:25,594
[SPEAKER_01]: The discrepancy of owning the unownable.

374
00:29:27,856 --> 00:29:30,138
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as there's trace of discrepancy, that's all.

375
00:29:30,578 --> 00:29:33,800
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way that's manifested is through suffering.

376
00:29:36,302 --> 00:29:39,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever there is suffering, means to that extent, there is discrepancy of craving.

377
00:29:40,565 --> 00:29:45,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a person who would say it is merely suffering... Well, means it's an ārya-sāvaka.

378
00:29:45,829 --> 00:29:48,731
[SPEAKER_01]: An ārya-sāvaka, but she's not... She's not an ārahant, yeah.

379
00:29:48,911 --> 00:29:49,532
[SPEAKER_00]: But she's not...

380
00:29:50,380 --> 00:29:52,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Really touched by suffering.

381
00:29:52,462 --> 00:29:56,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, she might be like almost close to nārānta or something like that, yeah.

382
00:29:57,966 --> 00:30:02,729
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason why she said that is because that's actually, as I said before, that's what's in common.

383
00:30:03,690 --> 00:30:11,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Even to a completely unenlightened pūtujana and somebody who is on the way to enlightenment, he's suffering and trying to overcome it.

384
00:30:12,817 --> 00:30:17,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Pūtujana tries to overcome his unpleasantness, this pleasure, by going for centrality.

385
00:30:18,167 --> 00:30:19,448
[SPEAKER_01]: By acting out of your will.

386
00:30:19,768 --> 00:30:21,749
[SPEAKER_01]: All that is rooted in you feeling the pain.

387
00:30:22,990 --> 00:30:24,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you don't want to feel the pain.

388
00:30:25,411 --> 00:30:28,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, suffering arises, suffering basically ceases.

389
00:30:28,893 --> 00:30:30,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything else you do is on account of that.

390
00:30:31,214 --> 00:30:31,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

391
00:30:33,075 --> 00:30:34,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's same for Ārya Savaka.

392
00:30:35,197 --> 00:30:40,279
[SPEAKER_01]: But now he knows that, so he doesn't go about it in a wrong way that will make that suffering worse.

393
00:30:41,320 --> 00:30:46,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Way of sensuality, ill will, distraction, carelessness, wrong speech and so on.

394
00:30:47,917 --> 00:30:55,241
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he realized that these things, doesn't matter, whether it was patujana or practicing for arahantship, it's all rooted in the same reason.

395
00:30:55,541 --> 00:30:56,522
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to suffer.

396
00:30:59,123 --> 00:31:09,168
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that's the goal of patujana, then you realize that your actions are causing a massive contradiction, because it's because of your actions you keep suffering even more.

397
00:31:09,829 --> 00:31:12,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only reason you act is because you don't want to suffer.

398
00:31:13,395 --> 00:31:16,216
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I meant, like, nobody in that talk, nobody wants sensuality.

399
00:31:16,797 --> 00:31:24,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Even people who are full-on of a view that sensuality is great and worth living for, they don't want it, that's why you go to it.

400
00:31:24,521 --> 00:31:26,982
[SPEAKER_01]: To try and satisfy... To try to get rid of it, yeah.

401
00:31:28,603 --> 00:31:31,965
[SPEAKER_01]: If sensuality is truly pleasant, there would be no desire in regard to it.

402
00:31:36,227 --> 00:31:37,167
[SPEAKER_00]: You're truly satisfying.

403
00:31:37,588 --> 00:31:37,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

404
00:31:42,373 --> 00:31:47,957
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's puthujjana or not, all is there is suffering in you acting out of it.

405
00:31:50,298 --> 00:32:04,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the people with wisdom are people who have recognized that certain way of acting out of suffering can possibly bring you closer to actually not suffering.

406
00:32:05,989 --> 00:32:10,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sensory strength, mindfulness, no distraction, no new world.

407
00:32:14,236 --> 00:32:20,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the point is to... it doesn't matter where you are in the... And even for an arahant, you can say, to an extent.

408
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:34,049
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, you know, I don't quite know, in terms of Pali, what exactly... what terms she used for that, but even from an arahant's point of view, it's not suffering anymore, but there is discomfort, there is disturbance.

409
00:32:34,109 --> 00:32:34,830
[SPEAKER_00]: He says dukkha.

410
00:32:35,530 --> 00:32:35,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

411
00:32:36,831 --> 00:32:39,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, usually dukkha was used for a non-arahant.

412
00:32:42,718 --> 00:32:52,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in other suttas and verses, when a person, when a monk becomes an arahant, he would say, oh, holy life has been lived, I've done my task, so everything is better.

413
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:54,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that should be abandoned has been abandoned.

414
00:32:54,861 --> 00:33:01,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever's left is basically a nuisance and discomfort that can arise on account of this still living body.

415
00:33:01,642 --> 00:33:01,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:33:04,063 --> 00:33:09,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's kind of a residue of an arahant, which is a residue of having a living body.

417
00:33:09,246 --> 00:33:16,790
[SPEAKER_01]: But, when this body breaks down, breaks apart, there will be no more suffering left, so to speak.

418
00:33:16,830 --> 00:33:26,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's that discomfort for an arahant, discomfort of the living body, discomfort of the things arising, that is the the whole world of suffering for putuja.

419
00:33:26,935 --> 00:33:28,016
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's rooted in the same.

420
00:33:29,156 --> 00:33:31,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Body exposed to elements and these various things that come your way.

421
00:33:33,930 --> 00:33:34,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, it's nothing.

422
00:33:35,111 --> 00:33:43,477
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a pile of aggregates, called creature, and discomfort manifested in them, enduring, ceasing, and so on.

423
00:33:44,298 --> 00:33:50,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, when Putujana is touched by that discomfort, he grieves, he suffers, he laments, he's... and that's his dukkha for him.

424
00:33:50,862 --> 00:33:53,304
[SPEAKER_01]: When Arhat is touched by that discomfort, there is no pain.

425
00:33:54,525 --> 00:33:56,006
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you speak objectively, yeah.

426
00:33:56,707 --> 00:34:00,369
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only dukkha that manifests within this thing that we call the creature.

427
00:34:02,432 --> 00:34:05,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever's felt, it's already discomfort.

428
00:34:07,258 --> 00:34:08,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a pressure, exactly.

429
00:34:09,361 --> 00:34:14,967
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for an arahant, he, for him, his attitude is whatever felt, whatever's felt counts as suffering.

430
00:34:15,288 --> 00:34:16,910
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he stops feeling everything.

431
00:34:31,575 --> 00:34:36,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, she probably wasn't an arahant yet, because Māra was still trying to find access to her.

432
00:34:39,157 --> 00:34:42,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for an arahant, it means basically Māra gave up on you.

433
00:34:43,178 --> 00:34:44,879
[SPEAKER_01]: But she was on the right direction.

434
00:34:44,899 --> 00:34:47,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, she was virtually there.

435
00:34:51,320 --> 00:34:54,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, thinking like, for example, creature is a useful reflection.

436
00:34:55,801 --> 00:34:57,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking of yourself as a creature first.

437
00:34:59,355 --> 00:35:02,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I'm a creature because I am, but I am because I'm creature.

438
00:35:03,616 --> 00:35:07,397
[SPEAKER_01]: As the verse describes, you're a pile of aggregates.

439
00:35:08,378 --> 00:35:09,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Pile of aggregates is called a creature.

440
00:35:10,258 --> 00:35:13,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there is a presence of a pile, creature is there.

441
00:35:14,719 --> 00:35:15,940
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, none of that is issue.

442
00:35:15,960 --> 00:35:20,741
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue is then you developing the ownership in regard to that whole thing.

443
00:35:21,421 --> 00:35:26,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether in regard to the pile of aggregates or in regard to the creature, either way, you develop a sense of ownership.

444
00:35:27,333 --> 00:35:38,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you contemplate that the necessary basis for any ownership, any sense of self, anything you feel, anything you crave on account of what you feel, the necessary basis for that is that this creature that I am is given.

445
00:35:39,998 --> 00:35:41,318
[SPEAKER_01]: The pile of aggregates is given.

446
00:35:43,179 --> 00:35:51,762
[SPEAKER_01]: That will basically contain any notion of self that you have, even... doesn't matter how independent you might feel, it will be seen as a...

447
00:35:52,948 --> 00:35:56,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Inseparable, impossible to step outside of the container of the creature.

448
00:35:57,252 --> 00:35:59,915
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the sutta says.

449
00:36:00,476 --> 00:36:10,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, for the future reappearances, for like in saṃsara, doesn't matter in what realm, for as long as there is craving there, the... that whole...

450
00:36:12,487 --> 00:36:13,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Mistaken.

451
00:36:13,789 --> 00:36:14,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

452
00:36:14,209 --> 00:36:16,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Notion of identity, ownership is there.

453
00:36:18,347 --> 00:36:20,168
[SPEAKER_00]: But whatever realm there is, there always...

454
00:36:20,468 --> 00:36:21,368
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a creature.

455
00:36:21,528 --> 00:36:22,888
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there is always the five aggregates.

456
00:36:22,968 --> 00:36:23,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the point of view.

457
00:36:23,809 --> 00:36:25,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Creature is the name for the five aggregates.

458
00:36:25,529 --> 00:36:33,471
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why I say it's good to, like, use creature as contemplation, because you say five aggregates seems technical, seems theoretical, seems out there.

459
00:36:33,971 --> 00:36:35,312
[SPEAKER_01]: All these five components.

460
00:36:36,992 --> 00:36:42,194
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, oh, your sense of self, self-view, well, that's a bit too close to be seen.

461
00:36:42,214 --> 00:36:44,054
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I am that sense of view.

462
00:36:44,854 --> 00:36:46,335
[SPEAKER_01]: But using the term creature,

463
00:36:47,412 --> 00:36:57,875
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of something you see that you are, but at the same time, the fact that you are a creature, you have no say in the way that creature manifested, in how that creature and you... how long that creature will be alive.

464
00:36:58,855 --> 00:37:04,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it kind of pertains to you, but it cannot be owned in the same sense.

465
00:37:04,177 --> 00:37:14,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you develop a context that you are a creature, you... you're basically developing a context of certain, eh... un-ownability.

466
00:37:15,450 --> 00:37:21,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Of your environment and your experience as a whole, of the five aggregates, you develop in the context of this passion.

467
00:37:23,796 --> 00:37:24,196
[SPEAKER_00]: To this?

468
00:37:24,837 --> 00:37:26,538
[SPEAKER_01]: To whatever you can experience, yeah.

469
00:37:26,878 --> 00:37:32,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because passion comes from that self-centeredness, from assuming that your sense of self is independent of the creature or the five aggregates.

470
00:37:35,184 --> 00:37:37,765
[SPEAKER_01]: But you are a creature first.

471
00:37:37,905 --> 00:37:38,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have sense?

472
00:37:39,807 --> 00:37:40,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Any sense?

473
00:37:40,787 --> 00:37:41,508
[SPEAKER_01]: How it manifests?

474
00:37:42,829 --> 00:37:43,870
[SPEAKER_01]: What is its lifespan?

475
00:37:45,267 --> 00:37:48,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just... any choices, decisions you make, are on account that you are It.

476
00:37:49,269 --> 00:37:49,930
[SPEAKER_01]: You are within It.

477
00:37:50,550 --> 00:37:51,351
[SPEAKER_00]: You are a being.

478
00:37:52,011 --> 00:37:52,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Hm.

479
00:37:52,511 --> 00:37:52,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Being.

480
00:37:53,492 --> 00:37:54,252
[SPEAKER_00]: A human being.

481
00:37:54,393 --> 00:37:55,093
[SPEAKER_00]: An animal being.

482
00:37:56,174 --> 00:37:59,616
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... your point of view is... it's... that's there.

483
00:37:59,956 --> 00:38:00,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Point of view is there.

484
00:38:00,936 --> 00:38:01,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Hm.

485
00:38:01,917 --> 00:38:02,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of view is the creature.

486
00:38:03,038 --> 00:38:03,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

487
00:38:04,158 --> 00:38:05,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it doesn't matter what realm.

488
00:38:05,219 --> 00:38:07,320
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always a point of view in every realm.

489
00:38:07,360 --> 00:38:11,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be... can you tell to that creature to not come anymore?

490
00:38:11,163 --> 00:38:12,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you tell to that creature to disappear?

491
00:38:13,304 --> 00:38:14,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you command it?

492
00:38:14,064 --> 00:38:14,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you control it?

493
00:38:15,425 --> 00:38:16,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you be the master of it?

494
00:38:16,905 --> 00:38:17,546
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

495
00:38:18,326 --> 00:38:21,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always the creature first.

496
00:38:21,186 --> 00:38:23,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Any notion of mastery, ownership, is within it.

497
00:38:26,868 --> 00:38:27,368
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

498
00:38:27,388 --> 00:38:29,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not about denying your sense of self.

499
00:38:29,228 --> 00:38:30,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about putting it in the right place.

500
00:38:32,149 --> 00:38:34,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh, what about... so, point of view, point of view.

501
00:38:37,270 --> 00:38:38,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a separate point of view?

502
00:38:39,331 --> 00:38:44,172
[SPEAKER_00]: A separate... a separate point of view that's not...

503
00:38:45,314 --> 00:38:47,696
[SPEAKER_00]: It's independent of anything else.

504
00:38:49,098 --> 00:38:52,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Can there be a point of view which doesn't view anything?

505
00:38:52,881 --> 00:38:53,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

506
00:38:53,542 --> 00:38:54,302
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

507
00:38:55,283 --> 00:38:57,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of view is defined by things that are being viewed.

508
00:39:00,889 --> 00:39:02,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, point of view is within the five aggregates.

509
00:39:02,931 --> 00:39:04,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of the five aggregates.

510
00:39:04,112 --> 00:39:04,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of view.

511
00:39:05,873 --> 00:39:06,534
[SPEAKER_00]: It determines everything.

512
00:39:06,594 --> 00:39:07,535
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it's not about.

513
00:39:09,155 --> 00:39:14,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting behind the point of view and entering, you know, the supreme realm of nibbāna or something.

514
00:39:15,078 --> 00:39:19,640
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about the choices you make on account of point of view and things that are being viewed.

515
00:39:21,361 --> 00:39:25,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about reaching the first point that will kind of... before which ignorance was not there.

516
00:39:28,104 --> 00:39:35,127
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not proliferating, not acting out of the unwholesome choices, not making the unwholesome

517
00:39:36,920 --> 00:39:44,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Actions, based on unwholesome intentions, they are fully within the point of view of what's being viewed, fully within the creature.

518
00:39:48,683 --> 00:39:50,924
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the practice is about.

519
00:39:52,504 --> 00:39:56,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness, responsibility for your actions and sense restraint.

520
00:39:58,486 --> 00:40:00,426
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is secondary to the point of view.

521
00:40:01,526 --> 00:40:03,667
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of the point of view and fix it.

522
00:40:04,751 --> 00:40:06,652
[SPEAKER_01]: As make it right, because it's not a matter of choice.

523
00:40:07,473 --> 00:40:12,516
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can stop proliferating things that are making it wrong, and that's how it becomes right, indirectly.

524
00:40:20,861 --> 00:40:26,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the same, you can't have a sense of self, unless you're not... unless you're not already there as a creature.

525
00:40:28,025 --> 00:40:32,488
[SPEAKER_01]: The way you have a reason given to yourself, to be owned, basically.

526
00:40:34,343 --> 00:40:50,288
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop maintaining the ownership of the creature, you are automatically diminishing the self-view, the view of the point of view being independent of everything else, being the first.

527
00:40:53,369 --> 00:40:54,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the origin.

528
00:40:54,390 --> 00:40:57,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the origin of things that are being viewed.

529
00:40:57,911 --> 00:41:03,733
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of everything's coming from me,

530
00:41:06,978 --> 00:41:07,919
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm within that.

531
00:41:09,279 --> 00:41:13,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm within whatever is presently enduring, in my experience.

532
00:41:13,381 --> 00:41:16,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever feeling is presently enduring, whatever intentions are presently enduring.

533
00:41:16,823 --> 00:41:17,924
[SPEAKER_01]: I am not.

534
00:41:18,184 --> 00:41:18,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

535
00:41:18,644 --> 00:41:19,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to do that.

536
00:41:19,285 --> 00:41:27,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no say in arising of intentions, but then you realize, oh, so you do have a say in choosing to entertain one type of intention on account of the other.

537
00:41:27,849 --> 00:41:29,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, some say, who chooses?

538
00:41:29,990 --> 00:41:30,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

539
00:41:30,831 --> 00:41:33,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's a, that's a, that's basically what Mahārāja asked, pretty much.

540
00:41:34,092 --> 00:41:34,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Who's choosing?

541
00:41:34,853 --> 00:41:35,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

542
00:41:36,265 --> 00:41:40,927
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you ask who, means you assume there is a person, independent of the choice, who then makes a choice.

543
00:41:40,947 --> 00:41:41,087
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

544
00:41:41,127 --> 00:41:42,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Person is the result of the choice.

545
00:41:44,009 --> 00:41:45,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, who is choosing is wrong.

546
00:41:45,990 --> 00:41:48,971
[SPEAKER_01]: You should say that who is there because of choosing.

547
00:41:50,632 --> 00:41:55,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when choosing ceases to be wrong, the who of the choice will also diminish.

548
00:41:56,955 --> 00:42:00,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all you have is pile of determinations, as the verse says.

549
00:42:00,977 --> 00:42:01,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Pile of choices.

550
00:42:03,268 --> 00:42:13,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, because you haven't been maintaining the notion of who through wrong choices, you get to see that it's only choices, not the who behind the choices.

551
00:42:14,656 --> 00:42:18,498
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're saying that the choice chooses.

552
00:42:18,538 --> 00:42:27,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Well... That's... see, that's true... that's correct to say in theory, but for Puthujjana that's not true.

553
00:42:28,265 --> 00:42:30,987
[SPEAKER_01]: He chooses, and he is the result of what he chooses.

554
00:42:32,142 --> 00:42:34,303
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he needs to start choosing rightly first.

555
00:42:35,144 --> 00:42:38,546
[SPEAKER_01]: That sense-restraint, sīla, virtue must come first.

556
00:42:39,567 --> 00:42:47,031
[SPEAKER_01]: There is absolutely no dhamma without it.

557
00:42:47,952 --> 00:42:54,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, who chooses is simply a wrong question that is rooted in the wrong choices.

558
00:42:58,436 --> 00:42:59,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what about free will?

559
00:42:59,897 --> 00:43:02,478
[SPEAKER_00]: This term gets thrown around.

560
00:43:02,779 --> 00:43:03,219
[SPEAKER_01]: What about?

561
00:43:03,239 --> 00:43:05,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, who wills?

562
00:43:05,940 --> 00:43:06,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's the same question.

563
00:43:07,041 --> 00:43:08,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, free will means choice.

564
00:43:09,883 --> 00:43:10,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

565
00:43:10,283 --> 00:43:11,143
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is first?

566
00:43:11,764 --> 00:43:15,886
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole question, who chooses, puts the notion of I am first.

567
00:43:16,827 --> 00:43:17,527
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a mistake.

568
00:43:17,627 --> 00:43:18,668
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a wrong question.

569
00:43:19,148 --> 00:43:20,008
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still ask it.

570
00:43:21,329 --> 00:43:25,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is that who chooses means there is who first that then makes a choice.

571
00:43:25,912 --> 00:43:27,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just need to find out who is that who.

572
00:43:29,196 --> 00:43:30,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's... that's a wrong order.

573
00:43:31,297 --> 00:43:32,558
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the choice that comes first.

574
00:43:32,638 --> 00:43:34,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of who is secondary to it.

575
00:43:36,159 --> 00:43:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you realize the question of who chooses becomes unintelligent.

576
00:43:43,323 --> 00:43:44,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Because choice comes first.

577
00:43:44,964 --> 00:43:45,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice is there.

578
00:43:46,064 --> 00:43:46,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice is there.

579
00:43:47,025 --> 00:43:48,986
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there is a sense of who in regard to it.

580
00:43:49,146 --> 00:43:50,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Because choice is owned.

581
00:43:54,128 --> 00:43:55,129
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop owning choice.

582
00:43:57,617 --> 00:43:59,759
[SPEAKER_01]: The question of who subsides, subsides.

583
00:44:01,740 --> 00:44:07,264
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what stops a person from choosing bad things?

584
00:44:07,304 --> 00:44:10,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Previous choices.

585
00:44:10,426 --> 00:44:12,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Bad choices are maintained through choosing badly.

586
00:44:13,328 --> 00:44:18,111
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop choosing badly, that's what Buddha meant by purifying your action beforehand.

587
00:44:18,131 --> 00:44:21,814
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, you know, the young man leaves their home out of faith, practices,

588
00:44:23,638 --> 00:44:28,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Dwells not welcoming centrality or will, lives restrained, secluded.

589
00:44:28,879 --> 00:44:34,641
[SPEAKER_01]: He gets to understand the dhamma, he develops three-fourth knowledge, he frees himself, because his actions have been purified beforehand.

590
00:44:38,221 --> 00:44:42,662
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what stops you from choosing bad, because you basically stop choosing badly.

591
00:44:43,162 --> 00:44:45,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And choosing bad needs maintenance of choosing badly.

592
00:44:46,551 --> 00:44:48,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Bad choices need to be there, maintained.

593
00:44:48,292 --> 00:44:49,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you stop choosing badly, it will fade away.

594
00:44:50,392 --> 00:44:55,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Same like... like any... you know, you can see that in the... in the... like on a worldly level, addictions.

595
00:44:56,554 --> 00:44:58,735
[SPEAKER_01]: They're depressing you to choose them over and over again.

596
00:44:59,115 --> 00:45:02,296
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when you free yourself from it, you can't choose it anymore.

597
00:45:02,776 --> 00:45:05,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's actually unpleasant, and now it's seen as unpleasant.

598
00:45:08,398 --> 00:45:09,938
[SPEAKER_01]: But before that, it was seen as pleasant.

599
00:45:11,917 --> 00:45:15,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Drug addictions, any other substance, any form of addiction, for that matter.

600
00:45:16,079 --> 00:45:18,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality, as an addiction, same.

601
00:45:19,700 --> 00:45:22,121
[SPEAKER_01]: With the sadist, because you don't know it's painful, that's where you go to it.

602
00:45:22,341 --> 00:45:24,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a man with electricity burning his skin with embers.

603
00:45:25,262 --> 00:45:26,143
[SPEAKER_01]: It was always painful.

604
00:45:27,543 --> 00:45:29,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Just, he thought it was pleasant.

605
00:45:29,604 --> 00:45:32,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Once he sees that it's painful, he cannot possibly go back to it.

606
00:45:36,707 --> 00:45:39,028
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why everything that is bad will be abandoned.

607
00:45:39,721 --> 00:45:47,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything that is bad requires that discrepancy of ignorance, requires its maintenance.

608
00:45:47,167 --> 00:45:50,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you stop providing that, it will cease without a reminder.

609
00:45:50,890 --> 00:45:51,611
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot come back.

610
00:45:51,651 --> 00:45:55,414
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot choose badly again, because there is no more basis for bad choices.

611
00:45:57,055 --> 00:46:02,099
[SPEAKER_01]: And basis for bad choices are bad choices that you stopped engaging with now.

612
00:46:02,460 --> 00:46:03,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you remove the basis for it.

613
00:46:08,901 --> 00:46:18,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people have fear that if I let go of my sense of self, then I'll just do whatever I do.

614
00:46:18,104 --> 00:46:19,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's because they haven't understood any of it.

615
00:46:20,885 --> 00:46:23,106
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't let go of your sense of self as a matter of choice.

616
00:46:23,966 --> 00:46:24,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no responsibility.

617
00:46:24,506 --> 00:46:29,888
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're fearing, yeah, if you're fearing that you will let go of your sense of self, you're taking that to be your sense of self.

618
00:46:34,065 --> 00:46:37,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Like if I let go of my responsibility for things.

619
00:46:38,146 --> 00:46:38,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

620
00:46:38,727 --> 00:46:40,267
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't let go of the responsibility.

621
00:46:42,188 --> 00:46:43,048
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only deny it.

622
00:46:44,829 --> 00:46:47,310
[SPEAKER_01]: The true letting go of responsibility is not a matter of choice.

623
00:46:48,410 --> 00:46:51,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a matter of undoing the symptoms of it.

624
00:46:51,791 --> 00:46:54,432
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

625
00:46:54,452 --> 00:46:55,753
[SPEAKER_00]: But responsibility remains.

626
00:46:56,653 --> 00:46:56,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

627
00:46:57,653 --> 00:46:58,654
[SPEAKER_01]: You just stop owning it.

628
00:46:59,414 --> 00:47:02,495
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to stop owning your responsibility is to first accept it.

629
00:47:04,085 --> 00:47:04,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

630
00:47:04,305 --> 00:47:06,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Responsibility is on the sense of self, as we spoke before.

631
00:47:07,787 --> 00:47:11,729
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep going around saying there is no self, there is no self, well, how can you undo that mistake?

632
00:47:14,351 --> 00:47:18,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, responsibility is the burden that needs to be rectified.

633
00:47:18,973 --> 00:47:21,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through denying it, but basically going beyond it.

634
00:47:22,755 --> 00:47:25,677
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot go beyond your responsibility for as long as you're avoiding it.

635
00:47:27,117 --> 00:47:28,478
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you have to accept responsibility.

636
00:47:28,498 --> 00:47:32,139
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the mundane right view has to come first.

637
00:47:32,539 --> 00:47:33,340
[SPEAKER_01]: There is mother.

638
00:47:33,500 --> 00:47:34,320
[SPEAKER_01]: There is father.

639
00:47:34,380 --> 00:47:36,121
[SPEAKER_01]: There are results of my actions.

640
00:47:36,281 --> 00:47:39,102
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there is the weight of my responsibility.

641
00:47:39,642 --> 00:47:53,888
[SPEAKER_01]: If you go living your life, even if you're a Buddhist practitioner, denying that very basic recognition of responsibility for what you choose to do, what you choose to say, how you choose to live your life, what you choose to think, it's on you.

642
00:47:54,508 --> 00:47:54,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully.

643
00:47:55,709 --> 00:47:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're refusing to accept that responsibility because it feels unpleasant, then wait.

644
00:47:59,852 --> 00:48:03,054
[SPEAKER_01]: You're refusing to basically partake in the mundane right view.

645
00:48:04,055 --> 00:48:06,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Good luck, then, developing the the supramundane one.

646
00:48:08,318 --> 00:48:08,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

647
00:48:09,359 --> 00:48:14,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way you can go beyond responsibility is through first fully accepting it and realizing there is no way out.

648
00:48:17,704 --> 00:48:18,385
[SPEAKER_00]: This creature...

649
00:48:19,897 --> 00:48:21,498
[SPEAKER_01]: It's responsible for its actions.

650
00:48:22,259 --> 00:48:26,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this creature is not free from its actions.

651
00:48:26,162 --> 00:48:27,343
[SPEAKER_01]: This creature is not an arahant.

652
00:48:30,806 --> 00:48:32,548
[SPEAKER_00]: This creature comes from mother and father.

653
00:48:32,868 --> 00:48:33,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

654
00:48:34,449 --> 00:48:40,234
[SPEAKER_01]: There is given, there is received, there is the unknown, there are the extents that I cannot fathom.

655
00:48:40,834 --> 00:48:42,716
[SPEAKER_01]: You admit all of that as there is a phenomenon.

656
00:48:44,718 --> 00:48:47,240
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to see where the problem is correctly.

657
00:48:49,025 --> 00:48:54,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason people will deny their responsibility, people will deny their sense of self, is because it's unpleasant to admit it.

658
00:48:55,109 --> 00:48:56,370
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

659
00:48:56,390 --> 00:48:59,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, it's only suffering that arises and suffering that endures.

660
00:48:59,652 --> 00:49:01,614
[SPEAKER_01]: And whatever you do is on account of that, avoiding it.

661
00:49:12,641 --> 00:49:16,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, yeah, the creature is a good, good term to think about.

662
00:49:18,987 --> 00:49:24,851
[SPEAKER_01]: As a necessary base for your experience as a whole, because that's... the creature is the name for the five aggregates, as the verse says.

663
00:49:26,613 --> 00:49:43,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Using the other Pali word, pūgala, individual, which was referred to, you know, arahant as well, as we said, it's kind of... it's... it's sort of less practical, because people already have lots of... oh, I'm an individual, he's an individual, you're an individual.

664
00:49:45,548 --> 00:49:50,271
[SPEAKER_01]: My individuality can kind of, through language, basically became appropriate.

665
00:49:50,611 --> 00:49:56,995
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean the same thing anymore, as Pūgala meant, when the Buddha used it to describe arahant, hari-sāvakas.

666
00:49:57,775 --> 00:49:59,816
[SPEAKER_01]: But creature is something, yeah, creature.

667
00:50:01,617 --> 00:50:03,619
[SPEAKER_01]: You are, you just are a creature.

668
00:50:03,739 --> 00:50:04,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

669
00:50:04,379 --> 00:50:05,780
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the five aggregates are.

670
00:50:05,800 --> 00:50:06,420
[SPEAKER_01]: They just are.

671
00:50:07,321 --> 00:50:09,282
[SPEAKER_01]: A pile of aggregates.

672
00:50:09,322 --> 00:50:09,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a heap.

673
00:50:11,123 --> 00:50:11,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Five heaps.

674
00:50:12,584 --> 00:50:13,144
[SPEAKER_01]: They just are.

675
00:50:13,424 --> 00:50:14,325
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in that.

676
00:50:14,783 --> 00:50:17,325
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot remove one, have the other one a bit more.

677
00:50:17,365 --> 00:50:18,646
[SPEAKER_01]: You just simply have no say in that.

678
00:50:18,806 --> 00:50:22,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable for you that you can alter or change their order or anything like that.

679
00:50:23,389 --> 00:50:26,671
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly the fact that you're a creature that's out of your hands.

680
00:50:29,173 --> 00:50:39,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not forgetting that in the practice of your mindfulness, that you're a creature first, creature in which creation you had no say in, yet now you're appropriating it.

681
00:50:39,620 --> 00:50:40,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that a right thing to do?

682
00:50:41,790 --> 00:50:50,912
[SPEAKER_01]: My sense of ownership of this is within this creature that's been, like, thrown at me, given to me, the way it is.

683
00:50:52,352 --> 00:50:56,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, my sense of ownership is not the ultimate owner of this, it's the result of it.

684
00:50:58,233 --> 00:50:59,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not the first, it's the second.

685
00:51:01,514 --> 00:51:03,274
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you undo the sense of I am.

686
00:51:04,354 --> 00:51:07,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by denying it, but by saying not I am.

687
00:51:08,295 --> 00:51:09,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am

688
00:51:10,428 --> 00:51:11,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Not is first.

689
00:51:11,448 --> 00:51:13,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not, I am.

690
00:51:14,849 --> 00:51:16,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Not is the first.

691
00:51:16,550 --> 00:51:18,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine is the second.

692
00:51:18,710 --> 00:51:20,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like going around saying, not mine, not mine.

693
00:51:22,111 --> 00:51:23,331
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when you have to be able to say this.

694
00:51:23,391 --> 00:51:24,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, comma, I am.

695
00:51:26,112 --> 00:51:26,912
[SPEAKER_00]: I am the second.

696
00:51:28,072 --> 00:51:29,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I am.

697
00:51:30,933 --> 00:51:37,975
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you you stop assuming I am stepping outside of that which is not yours.

698
00:51:37,995 --> 00:51:39,055
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not comes first.

699
00:51:39,820 --> 00:51:45,884
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is not mine, that cannot be mine, that is unownable, that is inconceivable.

700
00:51:47,144 --> 00:51:50,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the creature is.

701
00:51:50,366 --> 00:51:52,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Clarify what the creature is, you will stop owning it.

702
00:51:53,548 --> 00:51:56,290
[SPEAKER_00]: You will cease to be a person with personality view.

